1 INTRODUCTION
As vehicle automation advances, drivers are more likely to engage in non-driving related tasks (NDRTs) [1], which may compromise their situation awareness [4, 15] and ability to resume control when needed [2, 13]. This issue is particularly critical for SAE levels 2/3 automated vehicles (AVs)[16], where drivers must be able to safely recover control if the vehicle encounters operational design domain (ODD) limitations. Effective attention management, such as adequately splitting attentional resources between the driving task and NDRTs, is crucial for maintaining situation awareness [18, 22] and affects take-over performance during safety-critical scenarios [9, 11, 12, 14, 20]. Previous studies on this topic [6, 10] have primarily relied on gaze concentration metrics to assess drivers’ visual attention, providing insights into overall attention distribution patterns. However, these measures have limited capabilities in explaining safety-related scanning patterns and attention shifts, which are relevant in complex driving scenarios. Examining attention shifts can reveal how quickly and efficiently drivers re-focus their attention on critical
