road areas when necessary, offering additional insights into scanning behaviour and potential safety implications. Markov Chain analysis has emerged as a promising tool for understanding drivers’ gaze transitions and attention management strategies in various driving contexts. It has been successfully applied to investigate drivers’ hazard perception scanning patterns in both manual driving and vehicle automation [see 7, 19]. This approach models the probability of a driver’s gaze transitioning from one area of interest to another based on their current gaze location. By considering the temporal aspects of gaze transitions, Markov Chain analysis can provide valuable insights into drivers’ attention management strategies and identify patterns linked to safety-relevant behaviours and potential crash incidents [19]. While previous studies have applied Markov Chain analysis to investigate drivers’ hazard perception in manual driving and vehicle automation, its potential for examining attention management in the context of NDRTs and conditionally automated vehicles remains largely unexplored. In light of this research gap, we present the application of Markov Chain analysis in this context. Our approach seeks to provide novel insights into the utility of Markov Chain analysis, as well as the temporal aspects of drivers’ attention management strategies, complementing traditional gaze concentration metrics.
2 METHODOLOGY
We used an existing driving simulator study to showcase the application and utility of Markov Chain analysis in understanding drivers’ attention management strategies in conditionally automated vehicles with NDRTs—in particular, insights into the temporal aspects of drivers’ attention allocation and the potential to inform the future design of AVs interfaces. Below, we first describe the simulator study for context, followed by a description of the Markov Chain analysis.
2.1 Simulator Study
The dataset underlying the Markov Chain analysis is representative of other conditionally automated driving simulator studies, including a driving simulator with eye-tracking hardware with a range of defined areas of interest (AoIs), NDRTs (in this case, watching videos on different display modes: mobile phone vs. head-up display and baseline) and a driving scenario with automated driving and a take-over request (TOR).
2.1.1 Experiment Apparatus. The study employed an advanced driving simulator with a real-vehicle cabin, a surround-sound system, and a six-degree-of-freedom motion platform. Three frontview projection screens and LCD monitors for side and rear-view mirrors provided the driver with a 180-degree high-resolution field of view (cf. Figure 1). Eye-tracking data were collected using the Driver Monitoring System (PCDMS) developed by Seeing Machines Limited, which features an infrared camera and pulsed infrared lights. The PCDMS monitored drivers’ head and eye movements at a sample rate of 46 Hz during the automated drive (approximately 13 minutes). The extracted measurements included gaze yaw, gaze pitch, and the intersection of drivers’ gaze with 10 predefined AoIs.
Driving scenarios were created using real-world recorded videos of a manually driven car mimicking an automated vehicle’s performance [5, 8]. Three similar 15-minute trips were selected, each including inner-city roads, a major motorway, and suburban roads, with a simulated planned TOR issued at the exit of the motorway.
Figure 1: Advanced Driving Simulator.
2.1.2 Participants. Forty-six participants (23 males, 23 females; mean age 32.3 years, SD = 8.7) with valid driver’s licenses completed the study. Through social media posts, university website advertisements, and snowball sampling, participants were recruited from local communities.
2.1.3 Study Design and Procedure. The study employed a withinsubject design in which all participants underwent three 13-minute drives in an SAE Level 3 AV [16] simulator. Each drive, which was counterbalanced across the scenarios, included a planned TOR when exiting the motorway on the same route, and one of three counterbalanced NDRT engagement conditions: watching a video on a mobile phone, watching a video on a head-up display, and a baseline condition with no video. Drivers were allowed to position the mobile phone at any position they felt comfortable. Figure 2 displays the mobile and HUD conditions. The video was only shown during the automated driving phase. Lastly, a planned TOR was issued towards the end of the driving route when the vehicle exited the motorway.
