It is important to underscore that the analysis only covered drivers’ gaze during the periods that the automation was engaged (before the TOR was issued). This study focused specifically on drivers’ attention management strategies during the automated driving phase. The relationship between these strategies and takeover performance, though important, is beyond the scope of this work-in-progress. Prior to the analysis, the collected gaze data were filtered and validated to ensure that the “Unknown” AoI represented gaze fixations outside the predefined AoIs and did not include any missing data. The primary method employed in this analysis was Markov Chain analysis, which was used to model and understand drivers’ gaze transitions between different AoIs. The Markov Chain analysis generated a series of gaze fixation transition matrices for each participant, which contain the probability for each transition of fixations between AoIs to happen. These matrices were used to compare the fixation transition probabilities across conditions. By applying Markov Chain analysis to drivers’ gaze transitions, we aimed to gain insights into their attention allocation and scanning patterns under different NDRT conditions. To complement our analysis, Markov Chain analysis (i.e. gaze transition matrices) were used in parallel with drivers’ vertical/horizontal gaze dispersion. Fixations were calculated as a point of permanence of drivers’ gaze exceeding a minimum of 150 ms under a distance threshold of a 1-degree radius. The technique and thresholds for fixation detection are in line with standard protocols used in the field [17] and consistent with other studies in the field of gaze behaviour in automated driving [7, 12]. Gaze dispersion was calculated as the standard deviation of drivers’ gaze positions throughout their automated driving periods. Diverse statistical tests were used to compare the drivers’ gaze behaviour differences between the experimental conditions. ShapiroWilk tests were used to assess the dataset’s normality. For cases where normality was assumed, parametric statistical tests were conducted using ANOVA tests. Since the gaze transition probabilities generated by the Markov Chain analysis failed the normality tests, non-parametric Wilcoxon’s tests were used for statistical evaluation. An alpha value of 0.05 (95%) was used for all tests to assess the statistical significance. Individual comparisons between scenario conditions were evaluated by Bonferroni post-hoc tests. For the cases where non-parametric tests were applied, sample-paired Mann-Whitney U tests were used as post-hoc tests.
3 RESULTS
3.1 Gaze Dispersion
ANOVAs revealed significant effects of the experimental conditions on drivers’ vertical [F(2, 137) = 127.21, p < 0.001, ηp2 = 0.635] and horizontal gaze dispersion [F(2, 137) = 31.943, p < 0.001, ηp2 = 0.321]. The mobile condition showed the highest vertical dispersion (M = 10.913°, SD = 3.070°), while the HUD condition had the lowest horizontal dispersion (M = 9.628°, SD = 4.585°) (cf. Figure 3).
