4 DISCUSSION
Our analysis of drivers’ visual attention in an SAE level 3 automated vehicle revealed that both NDRT conditions (HUD and mobile) significantly impacted drivers’ gaze patterns. Both reduced lateral scanning and functional field of view, with the mobile condition causing higher vertical gaze dispersion away from the road. The Markov Chain analysis revealed that drivers in the mobile condition had a higher probability of shifting their gaze to the "Unknown" AoI and were less likely to return their gaze towards the "Centre Screen" after fixating on any AoI not related to the road environment. This suggests that handheld mobile NDRTs increase variability in drivers’ attention shifts, potentially compromising the occurrence of relevant gaze transitions for hazard perception and reducing their take-over readiness due to reduced situation awareness [3, 12, 21].
