Video analysis revealed that most drivers positioned their phones in locations that diverted their gaze from the road: 71% near the steering wheel and 28% on their lap. These locations fall within the "Unknown" AoI, indicating that drivers may not be attending to important driving-related information such as the road ahead, mirrors, or instrument panel. Thus, the mobile device location would be classified inside the “Unknown” AoI, according to the PCDMS world model. Although the HUD condition kept drivers’ gaze closer to the forward roadway, the Markov Chain analysis showed that drivers in the HUD condition were less likely to perform eventual checks on safety-relevant areas of interest compared to the baseline condition when deviating their gaze away from the "Centre Screen". This suggests that the HUD may lead to a narrower attentional focus on the NDRT, limiting drivers’ ability to effectively scan the driving environment. Markov Chain analysis provided a nuanced understanding of drivers’ attention management strategies that gaze dispersion measurements alone couldn’t capture. offers unique and valuable insights into drivers’ attention management strategies [see 7, 19]. These findings underscore the importance of considering detailed attention management when developing in-vehicle interfaces for conditionally automated vehicles, potentially informing the design of safer and more effective NDRTs.
5 CONCLUSION AND FUTURE WORK
Markov Chain analysis of gaze transitions offers a promising approach to understanding drivers’ attention allocation and informing the design of safer in-vehicle interfaces. By identifying the proportions and probabilities of gaze transitions between different areas of interest, this approach provides a nuanced and interpretable understanding of drivers’ gaze behaviour and attention management strategies. It is important to acknowledge the limitations of the current study. The findings are based on a simulator experiment, and while
the advanced driving simulator used in this study provides a high level of realism, it may not fully capture the complexities of realworld driving scenarios. Additionally, the study focused on a specific type of NDRT (watching a video) and a single level of vehicle automation (SAE Level 3). Future research should investigate the generalizability of these findings to a wider range of NDRTs, such as reading, typing, or engaging in conversation, as well as different levels of vehicle automation, including SAE Levels 2 and 4. Furthermore, future research should investigate optimal NDRT placements and the integration of driver monitoring systems to support safe interactions with NDRTs in AVs. The identified gaze transition patterns computed by Markov Chain analysis could guide the placement of critical information on HUDs and prompt drivers to scan safety-relevant areas periodically. Additionally, the potential generalisability of these findings to real-world driving scenarios and different levels of vehicle automation should be explored. Lastly, the potential long-term effects of NDRT engagement on drivers’ attention management strategies and the development of adaptive in-vehicle interfaces that respond to changes in driver attention should be explored. The preliminary findings of this study have significant implications for the AutomotiveUI community. Our research underscores the importance of carefully designing in-vehicle interfaces and NDRT interactions in conditionally automated vehicles. By demonstrating the impact of NDRT display modes on drivers’ attention management, we highlight the need to minimise potential negative effects on attention allocation and hazard perception capabilities. These preliminary findings contribute to the growing body of research on drivers’ attention management in conditionally automated vehicles. Our study demonstrates the value of Markov Chain analysis in understanding drivers’ gaze transitions and attention management strategies, opening up new avenues for research and development in the field of automotive user interfaces. The findings from our approach go beyond the acknowledgement of drivers’ attention focus to the NDRT through observation of their gaze dispersion. The
